WHY APACHE 2.0 RATHER THAN MIT
------------------------------

MIT has no patent language, so patent rights are at best implied. Apache
grants them expressly in section 3 and terminates the grant for a contributor
who brings a patent claim. That is a question an enterprise legal team asks
about an infrastructure dependency, and it sharpens with the beam, which pulls
in Noise, ICE and WebRTC.

Two smaller reasons point the same way. Section 6 states that the licence
conveys no trademark rights, which pairs directly with trademark being the
actual boundary here. Section 4(b) obliges a fork to state that it modified
the work.

Permissive to permissive, one copyright holder, and everybody gains a patent
grant they did not have.

